---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {funktorSidebar} from '../../../data/funktor-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout title="Messaging Module" sidebar={funktorSidebar} description="Email sending with pluggable backends">

    <h1>Messaging</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        Email sending with pluggable backends, sent message storage, and dev-friendly overrides.
    </p>

    <h2>Sending email</h2>

    <Code code={`val result = funktorMessaging.mailing.send(
    Email(
        source = "noreply@example.com",
        destination = EmailDestination.to("user@example.com"),
        subject = "Your order has shipped",
        body = EmailBody.Html {
            body { p { +"Your package is on the way." } }
        },
    )
)

if (result.success) {
    log.info("Sent email: ${'$'}{result.messageId}")
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Configuring a sender</h2>

    <p>Supply your provider and nothing else. Dev overrides, test-mode capture, persisting what was
        sent and the debug log are applied by the messaging module itself, so there is no chain to
        assemble and no step to forget.</p>

    <Code code={`val blueprint = kontainer {
    funktor(
        config = config,
        messaging = {
            useKarango()                       // persist sent messages

            // The ONE thing the app supplies. Built at most once, and never called at all
            // when the app runs in test mode.
            useSender(devConfig = config.devOverrides?.mailing) {
                AwsSesSender.of(config.aws.ses)
            }

            // Optional: your hooks run alongside the framework's, outside the dev overrides.
            onAfterSend { email, result -> metrics.record(result) }
        },
        // ...
    )
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <Aside>Do <strong>not</strong> register an <code>EmailSender</code> yourself. It is deliberately
        not a Kontainer service &mdash; the provider lives behind <code>useSender</code> so nothing can
        inject an undecorated sender and bypass the guarantees below. Send through
        <code>Mailing</code> (<code>funktorMessaging.mailing</code>).
    </Aside>

    <h2>Email senders</h2>

    <div class="not-prose my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <th class="py-2 text-gray-400">Sender</th>
                <th class="py-2 text-gray-400">Use case</th>
            </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <td class="py-2 text-white"><code>AwsSesSender</code></td>
                <td class="py-2 text-gray-400">AWS Simple Email Service. Production-grade delivery.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <td class="py-2 text-white"><code>SendgridSender</code></td>
                <td class="py-2 text-gray-400">SendGrid API. Alternative cloud provider.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <td class="py-2 text-white"><code>NullEmailSender</code></td>
                <td class="py-2 text-gray-400">No-op. Discards all emails silently.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                <td class="py-2 text-white"><code>OverridingEmailSender</code></td>
                <td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Wraps another sender and redirects all emails to a dev inbox.</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Dev overrides</h2>

    <p>Pass a <code>MailingDevConfig</code> to <code>useSender</code> and the module redirects every
        email to your dev inbox, prefixes the subject and adds a banner to the body. No accidental
        emails to real users.</p>

    <Aside>In production the real sender is used directly. In dev/staging all emails go to the address
        you configure, with the original recipient recorded in the send result.
    </Aside>

    <h2>Tests capture instead of sending</h2>

    <p>When <code>ktor.deployment.environment</code> is <code>test</code>, the module replaces your
        provider with a capturing sender &mdash; your <code>useSender</code> lambda is never invoked,
        so no client is built and no credentials are needed. Assert on what was sent by resolving
        <code>CapturedEmails</code>:</p>

    <Code code={`class MyMailSpec : FunktorApiSpec() {
    private val emails by service(CapturedEmails::class)

    init {
        "a password reset sends a mail with a usable link" {
            // ... trigger the flow ...
            val mail = emails.lastTo("user@example.com").shouldNotBeNull()
            val link = mail.hrefs().single()
        }
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <Aside>Read the mail from <code>CapturedEmails</code>, not from <code>SentMessagesStorage</code>:
        the persisted copy of an auth mail is anonymized, so the token is deliberately gone from it.
    </Aside>

    <h2>Auth email templates</h2>

    <p>The auth module's three transactional mails &mdash; account activation, password changed, password
        recovery &mdash; are rendered <strong>in Kotlin</strong>, one template class each, selected per
        locale.</p>

    <Code code={`abstract class AccountActivationEmailTemplate :
    LocalizedEmailTemplate<AccountActivationEmailTemplate.Params>() {

    data class Params(
        val recipient: EmailAddress,
        val senderEmail: String,
        val senderName: String,
        val applicationName: String,
        val activationUrl: String,
    )
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Each template declares its own <code>Params</code>, so a value that is renamed or forgotten is a
        compile error rather than a placeholder shipped to a recipient. Renderings live in a
        <code>Map&lt;Locale, (Params, Locale) -&gt; Email&gt;</code>, and a shared walker resolves
        <code>de-CH &rarr; de &rarr; en</code>. The renderer is <em>handed</em> the locale it matched, so the
        map key and the rendering cannot drift apart into a mixed-language mail.</p>

    <Aside>This started out as Markdown templates with <code>{'{{placeholder}}'}</code> substitution. It was
        built, reviewed, and thrown away &mdash; the review found a value that could be dropped from the args
        map and ship a literal placeholder to every recipient with the whole test suite green. Types do not
        have that failure mode.
    </Aside>

    <h3>Branding</h3>

    <p><code>EmailLayout</code> is the chrome around a template's content &mdash; colours, header, frame,
        footer. Supply one and all three mails use it:</p>

    <Code code={`val branded = EmailLayout { locale, content ->
    EmailBody.Html {
        body {
            div { +"…header…" }
            content()
            div { +footerFor(locale) }
        }
    }
}

AuthEmailTemplates.default(branded)`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>The layout receives the <strong>locale</strong> because a footer carries the imprint and unsubscribe
        line, and those have to be translated. A layout blind to the locale can only put an English footer
        under a German body. Replace an individual mail by subclassing its template and passing it alongside
        the layout.</p>

    <h3>What templates do not control</h3>

    <p>A template builds the <code>Email</code> and nothing downstream of it. The caller applies the storing
        policy and re-asserts the envelope, so a template cannot choose whether its own live token is
        persisted, nor add a cc that would deliver an activation link to a second mailbox.</p>

    <h2>Email hooks</h2>

    <p>Add hooks with <code>onAfterSend</code> in the <code>messaging</code> block. They run in the
        same place as the framework's own <code>StoringEmailHook</code> &mdash; outside the dev
        overrides, so they see the mail as your app composed it &mdash; and they still run under
        test. A hook buried inside the <code>useSender</code> lambda would do neither.</p>

    <h2>Sent message storage</h2>

    <p>Sent emails are persisted to a <code>SentMessagesStorage</code> via the storing hook, for any
        mail carrying an <code>EmailStoring</code> policy. Mail without one is not stored. Auth mails
        use <code>withAnonymizedContent</code>, which strips every link so a live token never
        reaches the database.
        Query by reference (e.g. user ID) to see what was sent and when.
        Storage backends: <code>useKarango()</code> for ArangoDB, <code>useMonko()</code> for MongoDB.</p>

    <div class="mt-12">
        <a href="/ultra/funktor/insights"
           class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-lg bg-brand-400/10 text-brand-400 border border-brand-400/30 hover:bg-brand-400/20 transition-all font-medium">
            Insights Module &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
